#   SSH for the partner - pass: the source of the rule is in zone WAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind WAN with --zone-network
#   Ping from the inside: the source of the rule is in zone LAN, DMZ, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind LAN, DMZ with --zone-network; an ICMP type, or a protocol other than TCP, UDP, ICMP and ICMPv6: the MX has no field for it
#   DNS from the firewall: the source of the rule is in zone LAN, GUEST, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind LAN, GUEST with --zone-network
#   NAT Web server HTTPS: the source of the rule is in zone WAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind WAN with --zone-network
#   NAT Application ports: the source of the rule is in zone WAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind WAN with --zone-network
#   Mail in: the source of the rule is in zone WAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind WAN with --zone-network
#   Web server over IPv6: the source of the rule is in zone WAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind WAN with --zone-network
#   Ping and errors to the firewall: the source of the rule is in zone WAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind WAN with --zone-network; an ICMP type, or a protocol other than TCP, UDP, ICMP and ICMPv6: the MX has no field for it
#   Administrators to the servers: the source of the rule is in zone LAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind LAN with --zone-network
#   Users to the web servers: the source of the rule is in zone LAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind LAN with --zone-network
#   Users to the web server by its outside address: the source of the rule is in zone LAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind LAN with --zone-network
#   Default allow LAN to any rule: the source of the rule is in zone LAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind LAN with --zone-network
#   Default allow LAN IPv6 to any rule: the source of the rule is in zone LAN, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind LAN with --zone-network
#   Servers fetch their updates: the source of the rule is in zone DMZ, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind DMZ with --zone-network; 'Updates' holds members only its platform knows
#   Mail out, not to the inside: not converted: negated destination that does not name the same on both sides of NAT rule 'Web server HTTPS' (198.51.100.10, 192.168.20.10); not converted: negated destination that does not name the same on both sides of NAT rule 'Application ports' (198.51.100.10, 192.168.20.11); the source of the r
#   Syslog to the collector: schedule 'OfficeHours' is not converted; the rule would apply at all times; schedule OfficeHours is not converted; the source of the rule is in zone DMZ, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind DMZ with --zone-network
#   Nothing from the list: references 'Blocklist': a URL table: its addresses are in a file on the firewall, not in the configuration
#   Guests browse: the source of the rule is in zone GUEST, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind GUEST with --zone-network
#   Phones of the guests: not converted: it matches a mark set by another rule (tagged) 'VOICE'; the source of the rule is in zone GUEST, and the MX names no zone: give the networks behind GUEST with --zone-network
